    <snippet id="django-views">
        <p>The <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/http/views/">Django documentation</a>
            define views as follows:</p>
        <tip>
            <p>A view function, or view for short, is a Python function
                that takes a web request and returns a web response.
                This response can be the HTML contents of a web page,
                or a redirect, or a 404 error, or an XML document,
                or an image or anything, really. The view itself
                contains whatever arbitrary logic is necessary to return
                that response.</p>
        </tip>
        <p>All the functions that take a request and return proper views (mainly html) are
            located in <code>views.py</code> files in app folders. We now review all the functions inside this app
            <code>views.py</code>.</p>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="django-forms">
        <p>According to <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/forms/">Django Documentation</a>:</p>
        <tip>
            <p>Unless you’re planning to build websites and applications that do nothing but publish content, and don’t
                accept input
                from your visitors, you’re going to need to understand and use forms.</p>
        </tip>
        <p>Forms are a means to get inputs from users and apply it into our product's logic. As %product% is a product
            that provides users the means for cryptocurrency trading, it is going to need get various inputs from the
            users
            in different contexts. Thus, we must use forms repeatedly and all of these are stored in
            <code>forms.py</code> files in each
            app's folder.</p>

    </snippet>

    <snippet id="django-model-forms-meta">
        <p>According to <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/forms/modelforms/">Django
            Documentations</a> Model forms require a <code>Meta</code> class
            so they would know which database table is corresponding to this form. This <code>Meta</code> class also
            provides some customization
            over the form such as excluding some fields to appear and be editable inside the form regardless of their
            presence in the database.
            For a more options refer to the <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/forms/modelforms/">Django
                Documentations</a>.</p>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="userena-secure-required-decorator">
        <list>
            <li><a href="https://django-userena.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api/decorators.html">secure_required</a>: A
                userena library decorator.
                We quote this function docstring in the following:
            </li>
        </list>
        <tip>
            <p>Decorator to switch an url from http to https.</p>
        </tip>
        <tip>
            <p>If a view is accessed through http and this decorator is applied to that
                view, then it will return a permanent redirect to the secure (https)
                version of the same view.</p>
        </tip>
        <tip>
            <p>The decorator also must check that <code>USERENA_USE_HTTPS</code> is enabled. If
                disabled, it should not redirect to https because the project doesn't
                support it.</p>
        </tip>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="csrf-exempt-decorator">
        <list>
            <li><a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/ref/csrf/#django.views.decorators.csrf.csrf_exempt">csrf_exempt</a>It
                is a django decorator which according to its docstring does the following:
            </li>
        </list>
        <tip>
            <p>Mark a view function as being exempt from the CSRF view protection.</p>
        </tip>
        <warning>
            <p>Be careful when to use this decorator as it has security risks. Read more on CSRF in <a
                    href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/ref/csrf/">Django's documentation</a>.</p>
        </warning>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="permission-required-or-403-decorator">
        <list>
            <li>
                <a href="https://django-guardian.readthedocs.io/en/stable/api/guardian.decorators.html#permission-required-or-403">permission_required_or_403</a>:
                A decorator from <a href="https://django-guardian.readthedocs.io/en/stable/overview.html">django-guardian</a>
                library. According to its docstring:
            </li>
        </list>
        <tip>
            <p>Simple wrapper for
                <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/auth/default/#django.contrib.auth.decorators.permission_required">permission_required</a>
                decorator.</p>
        </tip>
        <tip>
            <p>Standard Django's permission_required decorator redirects user to login page
                in case permission check failed. This decorator may be used to return
                <code>HttpResponseForbidden</code> (status 403) instead of redirection.</p>
        </tip>
        <tip>
            <p>The only difference between <code>permission_required</code> decorator is that this
                one always set <code>return_403</code> parameter to <code>True</code>.</p>
        </tip>

    </snippet>

    <snippet id="transaction-atomic-decorator">
        <list>
            <li>
                <a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/db/transactions/#controlling-transactions-explicitly">transaction.atomic</a>:
                Ensures the database operations are performed atomically.
            </li>
        </list>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="login-required-decorator">
        <list>
            <li><a href="https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.1/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator"><code>login_required</code></a>:
                According to its docstring:

        <tip>
            <p>Decorator for views that checks that the user is logged in, redirecting
                to the log-in page if necessary.
                </p>
        </tip>
        <p>It also takes login URL as an input parameter.</p>
        </li>
        </list>
    </snippet>

